  "result": "```js\nasync function getUser(id) {\n  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);\n  if (!rows.length) throw new Error('Not found');\n  return rows[0];\n}\n```\n\nKey changes:\n- `async` keyword on the function, return value becomes a `Promise` automatically\n- `await` replaces the callback, errors from `db.query` propagate as rejections naturally\n- Explicit error callback replaced by `throw`, which the caller catches via `try/catch` or `.catch()`\n\nCaller usage:\n\n```js\n// try/catch\ntry {\n  const user = await getUser(42);\n} catch (err) {\n  console.error(err.message);\n}\n\n// or .catch()\ngetUser(42).then(user => ...).catch(err => ...);\n```\n\nNote: this assumes `db.query` returns a Promise. If it's still callback-based, wrap it first with `util.promisify(db.query.bind(db))` or a manual `new Promise(...)`.",
